Z||000000||FIEROCIOUS||05-08-2000||12:44 AM||||Hey fiero guys and gals long time no type! (computers been down!) <BR>Has anyone removed the radiator overflow bottle?<BR>Will the Fiero overheat?<BR>What alternative bottles can be used if they do overheat? Like the ones from summit?||||reg||5
Z||000001||Frank R||05-08-2000||12:56 AM||||My overflow has been gone for a couple of years.  This device serves to recover coolant when a/c has been operating or the engine is very warm.  My engine never gets overly hot.  I have a manual fan switch and a 180 degree t-stat.  I'm going to fabricate one of aluminium soon.  The sunlite makes the plastic very brittle.....Frank R.     ||||reg||6
Z||000002||FIEROCIOUS||05-08-2000||01:03 AM||||Hey thanks Frank R., I actualy had you in mind when I was thinking about it! I took mine off a few days ago.<BR>Today it was spewing alot off coolant I bet my cap just happened to go bad at the same time.<BR>I thought about it, and the coolant can't go back into the radiator so it serves only as a catch can right? ||||reg||1
Z||000003||Gokart||05-08-2000||06:22 AM||||Just had to swap mine with the one in my parts car Saturday cause it had a crack in it. Funny, cause when I was walking toward my car Friday after work I'm looking at a small puddle someone showed me under the bra and I said "the engines in the back, not my fluid"! I shouldn't have been so cocky! Oh well, parts car pays off again  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><p>[This message has been edited by Gokart (edited 05-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000004||Phil||05-08-2000||07:02 AM||||Fierocious<BR>The recovery can does more than act as just a catch can. If your system is operating properly, when the coolant is hot and expands the excess flows through the rad. cap and into the overflow -as the system cools and the coolant contracts and makeup coolant is drawn from the catch can through the rad. cap back into the the rad. That's the reason the tube into the overflow can is in the bottom||||reg||1
Z||000005||FIEROCIOUS||05-08-2000||02:08 PM||||If the pressure of the cap is 15psi how does the coolant flow (back) into the radiator?<P>I removed the overflow bottle to give more room for airflow if it truely is needed I will reinstall it. <P>All the racecars I've ever had I've taken the overflow off (weight saveings of one ounce) LOL! they have been fine.<P>However we all know the Fiero is a differant animal in more than many respects from a "NORMAL" car thats why we love and drive them!  ||||reg||1
Z||000006||2birds||05-08-2000||04:03 PM||||Why would you want to remove the coolant expansion tank?  It weighs almost nothing, and I can't imagine it blocking any real airflow.  Its purpose is to provide expansion volume for the non-compressible water in your coolant system, and to provide for makeup volume as the fluid cools and contracts.  You run the risk of overpressurizing your coolant system, and pushing out the coolant you do have, then sucking air back in if you remove it.  The 15# radiator cap just allows pressure to build up before the coolant goes into the expansion tank, since water boils at a higher temp when pressurized.  I live in the Deep South, and I still don't see temps go above 170, unless I'm sitting in traffic, then the fan kicks on at around 225 (by the factory temp meter).  Never changed in 12 years, except when the thermostat occasionally sticks open.  I have the stock replacement 195 degree thermostat.  ||||reg||1
Z||000007||FIEROCIOUS||05-08-2000||05:12 PM||||But how does the coolant get "sucked" back into the radiator?<P>The radiator cap only allows the coolant to flow if it is above 15 psi. So how does the coolant out side the radiator become 15 psi?<P>If the radiator expels coolant it is because there is not enough room for the non compressable fluid at 15 psi when the radiator cap is bellow 15 psi it closes.<P>How does it "suck" fluid or air for that matter back in?<P>If it worked the way I'm thinking it would acctualy be a vacume after the coolant/radiator cooled does the radiator cap allow atmostpheric pressure in?<BR>I know I am wrong I would like to know why? and How?   ||||reg||1
Z||000008||2birds||05-08-2000||05:44 PM||||You're 100% on the mark about it being in a vacuum as it cools(contracts).  That's how it sucks the coolant back in.  The cap on the radiator acts like a one-way/check valve.  It allows the pressure to build up first before it relieves to the expansion tank.  This gives you the higher boiling point and maybe even prevents water pump cavitation by providing some net positive suction head to the impeller. ||||reg||1
Z||000009||Phil||05-08-2000||10:04 PM||||Just put you faith in the hands of powers that are. Coolent will flow back through the cap. ||||reg||1
Z||000010||Old Lar||05-08-2000||10:15 PM||||Mr Goodwrench still has them as a stock item.  I paid $8.69 for a new one (Fiero store $12.95) and installed it last week.  It was a bear getting my pudgy hands in position to remove one of the retaining nuts.<P>They do serve a purpose.||||reg||5
Z||000011||theogre||05-08-2000||10:35 PM||||The cap is a 2 way valve it opens for out flow at whatever pressure it was made for. It has a second opening in it for inflow.<P>In other words the coolant gets sucked back in thru the little brass valve in the center of the cap...... the little brass disk is spring loaded. It's not there to hold the rubber washer.<P>the catch bottle is a required item for proper operation. It insures no air enters the system as it cools. Air in the system is a bad thing, especially in Fiero. <P>It also catches the rather toxic Ethelyne Glycol and prevents it from killing things, like any pet that drinks it, and they will drink it. It tastes good to many animals. Kills grass good as well.<p>[This message has been edited by theogre (edited 05-08-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000012||Jay||05-08-2000||10:46 PM||||A word of advice, don't over tighten the nuts, that's what causes them to crack.  Just put it on snuggly, no need to reem it on!!||||reg||1
Z||000013||2birds||05-09-2000||12:40 AM||||You tell 'em, Ogre!||||reg||1
Z||000014||Frank R||05-09-2000||02:25 AM||||I must clarify my earlier post.  I have been using my Fiero for a long while without any recovery tank.  To further explain, the cooling system is engineered to be able to be operated under very heavy heat loads.  The system has a pressure cap to raise the boiling point of the coolant.  The boiling point is raised 2 degrees for every pound applied to the system.  As 16 pound cap raises boiling temps 32 degrees to [212 + 32<BR>= 244].  This affords a smaller radiator [read less costly to mfg].  The Fiero is unique in as much as the radiator is also not at the highest part of the cooling system.  When the heat load is high, high pressures develope, and the coolant is forced into a reservoir so as to be able to be returned to the cooling system.  After the pressure becomes reduced and the system cools more,the system creates a vacumn to draw the coolant back into the radiator.  Driving without a/c or high engine heat loading this recovery system does nothing.  But if you should make lots of heat the system is valuable.  I'm fabricating an aluminium coolant reservoir, and I can't see contents.   Frank R...||||reg||6
Z||000015||FIEROCIOUS||05-11-2000||02:30 AM||||I reinstalled the coolant overflow tank, For the record the car never did over heat however, when I opened the cap it contained Green foam!<P>The part # for the over flow tank is 10036923 (Thanks Kidz Wheelz) <P>Durring all this cooling fun I broke my THIRD 160 thermostat I still don't know why, well instead of blowing my hard earned cash on another one I took a stock cheapie (4.95) and took it apart then took it to a local radiator shop and had them soider the bracket to the thermostat. They did it for free.<P>I think Hypertech's bracket was just flemsy. <P>All the thermostats I've recieved from Hypertech have been backwards this may have atributed to the problem also? <P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||Phil||05-11-2000||06:43 AM||||Are you running a 160 thermostat with the stock computer chip?<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000017||Spyder||05-11-2000||11:22 AM||||I moved my recovery tank to the rear so it would be in the highest point, using an after market recovery tank. I moved it to where the battery was and moved the battery to the front. I replaced the clear plastic hose that came with it with fuel hose because it got too warm, when the coolant needed to come back in the hose would collapse with the suction not allowing the coolant to return. ||||reg||1
Z||000018||Spyder||05-11-2000||11:39 AM||||Also another way to increase the cooling is to cut a few 2-3” holes behind the radiator in the panel that separates the radiator and spare tire. This allows more air to flow through the radiator.||||reg||1
Z||000019||FIEROCIOUS||05-12-2000||12:48 PM||||Were does the air go after you drill the holes? I would think you would have no air flow. Were did you tap the lines to get the over flow in the rear?||||reg||1
Z||000020||theogre||05-12-2000||06:29 PM||||I doubt that a couple holes, unless bery large, in the divider would help. Especially if you aren't doing something to allow the air to exit the spare area.<P>A lower temp fan switch or manual fan lock will do much more than the holes. Adding hood openings of some sort, as some have done, would also make a differance. Hood openings also help eliminated headlight popup at high speed acording to those whove done it. Depends just how the openings are done.||||reg||1
Z||000021||Spyder||05-12-2000||08:35 PM||||The holes are recommended by V8 Archie when doing a V8 install, I thought it would help a V6 or 4 cylinder out.<BR>I thought the same thing about the air with nowhere to go so I went a step further. I removed the cowl drains to let the air flow up toward the windsheild and also through the internal channels of the car itself. Sorry I should have mentioned it.<BR>I used and adapter from summit that has the hole for the overflow hose.||||reg||1
